Describe the secure world in u-boot's own control device tree.

Applied to the rockpi4c-ftpm variant only: it is the one that packs OP-TEE as
BL32, and the others have no secure world to describe.

This patch reserves nothing and configures no memory: it only adds two nodes to
the device tree u-boot runs on, so u-boot's driver model can bind to firmware
that is already there.

  /firmware/optee  is what drivers/tee/optee probes on. Without it u-boot never
                   opens a TEE context, so nothing that goes through OP-TEE --
                   the fTPM, RPMB secure storage, the OP-TEE RNG -- comes up.
                   u-boot also copies this node into the kernel's device tree
                   via EFI_DT_FIXUP_PROTOCOL, so it is how the kernel learns
                   OP-TEE exists on the non-UKI boot path.

  /tpm             the fTPM, matched by "microsoft,ftpm". The TA is linked into
                   the tee.bin this variant loads, so the node describes
                   something that is genuinely there, and CONFIG_TPM2_FTPM_TEE
                   builds the driver that binds to it.

The nodes go in rk3399-u-boot.dtsi, which rk3399-rock-pi-4c-u-boot.dtsi reaches
through rk3399-rock-pi-4-u-boot.dtsi, rather than in the vendored upstream board
.dts under dts/upstream/ -- that subtree tracks devicetree-rebasing and should
not be edited downstream.

What the kernel needs is a *reservation* of the DRAM OP-TEE owns, and that is a
separate thing entirely: see overlays/rockpi4c-ftpm/rk3399-rock-pi-4c-optee.dtso,
which is merged into the measured UKI device tree.

diff --git i/arch/arm/dts/rk3399-u-boot.dtsi w/arch/arm/dts/rk3399-u-boot.dtsi
--- i/arch/arm/dts/rk3399-u-boot.dtsi
+++ w/arch/arm/dts/rk3399-u-boot.dtsi
@@ -16,6 +16,17 @@
 		u-boot,spl-boot-order = "same-as-spl", &sdhci, &sdmmc;
 	};

+	firmware {
+		optee {
+			compatible = "linaro,optee-tz";
+			method = "smc";
+		};
+	};
+
+	tpm {
+		compatible = "microsoft,ftpm";
+	};
+
 	pmusgrf: syscon@ff330000 {
 		compatible = "rockchip,rk3399-pmusgrf", "syscon";
 		reg = <0x0 0xff330000 0x0 0xe3d4>;
